eturn21search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month;     10.1002/9780470693711 ...     Wiley ... Betamax), organizational norms (formal vs. casual culture), driving conventions (left-hand vs. right-hand traffic), measurement units (metric vs. imperial), and network architectures all exhibit this same structure, as Lewis (1969) developed in his philosophical analysis of convention as the general solution to recurrent coordination problems.】\n\n# Convention¶\n\nLewis, D. (1969). Convention: A Philosophical Study. Harvard University Press.\n\nType\n    Book\nIntellectual base\n    Review or monograph\nYear\n    1969\nDOI\n    10.1002/9780470693711\nISBN\n    9780631232568\nDOI registrant\n    Wiley\nLink\n    https://doi.org/10.1002/9780470693711\nCited from\n    cognitive_science, economics_finance, information_theory, linguistics_semiotics\n\n## Cited by¶\n\n5 citations across 5 artifacts.\n\nEach citation links to the sentence it supports in the citing article.\n\n### Primes¶\n\n  * Arbitrariness of Symbolic Conventions\n    * This source Analyzes convention as an arbitrary, self-perpetuating solution to a recurrent coordination problem, sustained by mutual expectation, common knowledge, and salience across linguistic, social, and institutional domains.\n\n  * Coordination Problem and Equilibrium Selection\n    * 【7†The signature is robust across institutional and technological domains: technology standards (VHS vs. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBRIAN SKYRMS Signals: Evolution, Learning, and Information Brian SkyrmsSignals: Evolution, Learning, and InformationNew York: Oxford University Press, 2010, 208 pp., £35 (hardback) ISBN: 978-0199580828 | The British Journal for the Philosophy of Science: Vol 64, No 4 (https://www.journals.uchicago.edu/doi/10.1093/bjps/axt004)\nciteturn21search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.4 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Skyrms proposes that a signal’s informational content is a vector with components that record how much the receipt of the signal changes the probabilities of the possible states of nature having occurred. ... On the basis of this game, Skyrms ([2010]) argues that, contrary to Kant, universal deception is not only logically consistent but also ‘evolutionarily consistent in the sense of being an equilibrium’ (p.\nThe book unfolds in three major pieces. The first piece (Chapters 1–3 and 6) presents a new characterization of the informational content carried by a signal. Skyrms proposes that a signal’s informational content is a vector with components that record how much the receipt of the signal changes the probabilities of the possible states of nature having occurred. In a signalling game with two states, the content of signal A is the vector:\n\nImage\n\nThis definition of content has at least two desirable qualities. First, it is parsimonious with standard information theory. If one takes a weighted sum of the components in a content vector, the result is the Kullback–Leibler divergence, which is an established measure of the distance between two probability distributions. Here, these two distributions are the unconditional probabilities with which the states occur, and the probabilities of the states conditional on the receipt of the signal. The greater the divergence between these two distributions, the more information the signal carries about the state. Second, this definition makes informational content a generalization of propositional content. If a state’s probability conditional on a signal is zero, then the corresponding entry in the content vector will be −∞. This means that it is possible to read off a signal’s propositional content—the set of states ruled out by that signal—by looking for entries in the content vector that are −∞.\n\nThis definition of content is put to provocative work in Chapter 6 where it figures into a new naturalistic account of deception. According to this definition, a deceptive signal is one that raises the probability of a state that is not actually the case (this increase is reflected by a positive entry in the signal’s content vector) and solicits a response that benefits the sender, perhaps at the receiver’s expense. Skyrms presents games in which at equilibrium players signal in this way, and he even constructs a game in which at equilibrium every signal is deceptive. On the basis of this game, Skyrms ([2010]) argues that, contrary to Kant, universal deception is not only logically consistent but also ‘evolutionarily consistent in the sense of being an equilibrium’ (p. 82). There is likely much philosophical hay to be made over this argument, but at the very least we see this new characterization of deception, and the notion of informational content that underpins it, as an important contribution that should assist in uniting philosophical theories of content with the operationalized definitions of deception used by scientists studying animal behaviour.\n\nThe second major piece of Signals (Chapters 4–5 and 7–10) is an examination of learning and evolution in standard Lewis signalling games. In basic models of learning and evolution, players will sometimes fail to learn an efficient signalling system if either the world has more than two possible states or these states are not equiprobable. Much space is devoted to an investigation of mechanisms that enable players to learn to signal efficiently even in these adverse circumstances. One mechanism in particular, the invention of new signals, merits special mention. The extraordinary feature of this model is that it enables players to change the rules of the game they are engaged in. In standard game-theoretic analysis, the players’ strategy sets, and therefore also their vocabularies of potential signals, are fixed; these sets are stipulated by the modeller. But here we have players who can learn to expand their available actions over time. As they learn, the players literally alter the game they’re playing. This is something of a breakthrough in game-theoretic analysis; to our knowledge there is only one other model in which players can modify their action spaces.^{1}\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSkyrms, B. (2010) Signals Evolution, Learning & Information. Oxford University Press, Cambridge, MA. - References - Scientific Research Publishing (https://www.scirp.org/reference/referencespapers?referenceid=1268180)\nciteturn21search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 12.2 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Skyrms, B. (2010) Signals: Evolution, Learning & Information. ... http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199580828.001.0001 ... Furthermore, we applied signaling games in combination with reinforcement learning to show that results can be even more improved if cells learn to signal for navigating the behavior of neighbor cells.\n\n#### Article citationsMore>>\n\nSkyrms, B. (2010) Signals: Evolution, Learning & Information. Oxford University Press, Cambridge, MA.\nhttp://dx.doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199580828.001.0001\n\nhas been cited by the following article:\n\n  * TITLE: The Game of Life, Decision and Communication\n\nAUTHORS: Roland Mühlenbernd, Simon Schulz\n\nKEYWORDS: Game of Life, Signaling Games, Reinforcement Learning\n\nJOURNAL NAME: Natural Science, Vol.6 No.13, August 25, 2014\n\nABSTRACT: The game of life represents a spatial environment of cells that live and die according to fixed rules of nature. In the basic variant of the game a cell’s behavior can be described as reactive and deterministic since each cell’s transition from an actual state to a subsequent state is straight-forwardly defined by the rules. Furthermore, it can be shown that the alive cells’ spatial occupation share of the environment decreases quickly and levels out at a really small value (around 3%), virtually independent of the initial number of alive cells. In this study we will show that this occupation share can be strongly increased if alive cells become more active by making non-deterministic sacrificial decisions according to their individual positions. Furthermore, we applied signaling games in combination with reinforcement learning to show that results can be even more improved if cells learn to signal for navigating the behavior of neighbor cells. This result stresses the assumption that individual behavior and local communication supports the optimization of resourcing and constitute important steps in the evolution of creature and man.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDavid Lewis (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy) (https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/david-lewis/)\nciteturn21search9 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; David Lewis’s first book was Convention (1969a; note that all citations are to works by David Lewis, unless explicitly stated otherwise).\n## 2. Convention\n\nDavid Lewis’s first book was Convention (1969a; note that all citations are to works by David Lewis, unless explicitly stated otherwise). It was based on his Harvard Ph.D. thesis, and published in 1969. The book was an extended argument that language could be entirely grounded in convention. There is a simple argument that this could not be so. Conventions are formed by agreement, agreements are made in language, so language must precede convention, not be grounded in convention. (In the foreword to Convention, Quine appears to express some sympathy for this argument.) Lewis’s response is to deny that conventions require anything like an agreement. Rather, on his view, conventions are regularities in action that solve co-ordination problems. We can stumble into such a regularity without ever agreeing to do so. And such a regularity can persist simply because it is in everyone’s best interest that it persist.\n\n### 2.1 Analysis of Convention\n\nLewis viewed conventions as solutions to co-ordination problems (see Section 3.2 of the entry on convention). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPhilosophy of Information | Informationist Scientific Metaphysics: Bib: Information, SR, NOSR, QFT, Physics Pt 3. (https://brucerlong.blogspot.com/p/blog-page.html)\nciteturn21search10 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 days ago;     @book{skyrms-signals:-2010-1,\n\nSkyrms, B.  | Signals: evolution, learning, & information [BibTeX]  | 2010  |    | book  |  \nBibTeX:\n    \n    @book{skyrms-signals:-2010-1,\n      author = {Skyrms, Brian},\n      title = {Signals: evolution, learning, & information},\n      publisher = {Oxford University Press},\n      year = {2010}\n\nSkyrms, B.  | Signals: evolution, learning, and information [BibTeX]  | 2010  |    | book  |  \nBibTeX:\n    \n    @book{skyrms-signals:-2010,\n      author = {Skyrms, Brian},\n      title = {Signals: evolution, learning, and information},\n      publisher = {Oxford University Press},\n      year = {2010}\n\nPande, V.S.  | Simple theory of protein folding kinetics [Abstract] [BibTeX]  | 2010  | Physical Review Letters\nVol. 105(19), pp. 198101   | article  |  \nAbstract: We present a simple model of protein folding dynamics that captures key qualitative elements recently seen in all-atom simulations. The goals of this theory are to serve as a simple formalism for gaining deeper insight into the physical properties seen in detailed simulations as well as to serve as a model to easily compare why these simulations suggest a different kinetic mechanism than previous simple models. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConvention: A Philosophical Study | Wiley (https://www.wiley.com/en-au/Convention%3A%2BA%2BPhilosophical%2BStudy-p-9780631232568)\nciteturn21search11 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; David Lewis ... https://www.wiley.com/en-au/convention-a-philosophical-study-p-9780631232568\n\n## Convention: A Philosophical Study\n\nDavid Lewis \n\nISBN: 978-0-631-23256-8\n\